    "case_id": "218",
    "case_summary": "A 36-year-old man with no prior medical history presented with a 4-year history of diffuse bone pain around the knees and elbows and intermittent generalized pruritus. Over the past 2 months, his bone pain worsened and he developed blurred vision, bilateral exophthalmos, and difficulty walking. Previous workups at multiple centers were nondiagnostic; he had been treated intermittently with analgesics, vitamin D, topical steroids, and antihistamines.\n\nOn admission, he appeared well and was hemodynamically stable. Laboratory studies showed:\n• White-cell count and differential: normal  \n• Hemoglobin: normal  \n• Platelet count: mild thrombocytosis (120 × 10^9/L)  \n• Renal and liver function tests: normal  \n• Tumor markers and autoimmune serologies: negative  \n• C-reactive protein and erythrocyte sedimentation rate: mildly elevated  \n\nRadiographic studies demonstrated:\n• Knee radiographs: bilateral symmetric sclerotic changes of the distal femora and proximal tibiae  \n• Humeral radiographs: intramedullary sclerosis of the distal humeri  \n• Forearm radiographs (lateral view): sclerosis of the proximal ulnae and radii without fracture  \n\nNuclear and cross-sectional imaging revealed:\n• 99mTc bone scintigraphy and FDG-PET: bilateral symmetric uptake in long bones, right clavicle, and mandible, with uptake in subcutaneous and muscle tissues of the lower limbs  \n• CT and MRI of the lower limbs: multifocal intramedullary sclerosis of the femora and tibiae  \n• MRI of the brain: enhancing lesions in the pituitary gland and retrobulbar soft tissues  \n• PET/CT of the brain: corresponding FDG-avid lesions  \n• CT and PET/CT of the abdomen: bilateral perinephric soft-tissue thickening with FDG uptake  \n\nA CT-guided biopsy of the left proximal tibia showed diffuse infiltration by foamy histiocytes and fibrosis with rare Touton-like giant cells. Immunohistochemical staining of the histiocytes was positive for CD68, CD163, and factor XIIIa, and negative for CD1a, S100, and langerin; no granulomas or malignant cells were identified.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Erdheim-Chester disease\nB. Langerhans cell histiocytosis was excluded because “the histiocytes were … negative for CD1a … and langerin\nC. Rosai\nD. Granulomatous disorders (e.g., Wegener’s granulomatosis, neurosarcoidosis) were excluded because there were “no\nE. Malignant neoplasia was excluded because the biopsy showed “no … malignant cells” and was “negative for malignancy\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
